balloon’s envelope allows expansion. The balloon climbs because the inside air is still *warmer than the outside air at that altitude*, not because it’s staying as hot as it was at the surface. In your house, you’ve basically built a miniature, ceiling‑capped version of the atmosphere. The same physics (“hot air rises”) is at work in both places. The only reason your loft is roasting while the stratosphere is freezing is that your house stops the rising air before it can expand and cool very much. So, yes, the fact that upstairs is warmer is fantastic proof that hot air rises—and together with the sky getting colder, it actually shows the complete picture of what happens to that rising air when it has room to stretch out and lose its heat.
