01:10:10 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Great question! A lake or ocean seems like a perfect open system—no ceiling, just like the sky. Yet the warm water does stay on top, and cold water sinks to the bottom. That really does look like “heat rises” on its own. But here’s what’s actually happening: it’s still the **warm water (the fluid) that’s buoyantly floating**, not heat defying gravity. And this difference is exactly why the sky gets colder while the lake surface stays warm. ### Water stratification: buoyancy again - Water, like air, expands slightly when heated. That means a litre of warm water has a tiny bit less mass (lower density) than a litre of cold water. - Gravity pulls more strongly on the denser cold water, so it sinks, squeezing the lighter warm water upward. So the warm layer ends up on top. - This is purely **buoyancy of the fluid**, driven by density differences—identical to a hot-air balloon or the rising air above a radiator. In a lake, there’s no “rising” needed because the warm water is *already* at the top most of the time. Sunlight heats the surface directly; the warm water is less dense, so it just sits there, floating on the colder, denser water. If you inject warm water at the bottom, it would indeed rise, just like hot air—but in a natural lake, the heating happens at the top. ### Why doesn’t the warm water cool as it “rises”? Because in a lake the warm water doesn’t move up much—it’s already at the top. And even if a blob of warm water were to rise from deep down, the pressure change in water is enormous but water is nearly **incompressible**. It doesn’t expand significantly when pressure drops, so it undergoes almost no “adiabatic cooling.” Air, being a compressible gas, expands and cools dramatically as it rises and pressure decreases. That’s the critical difference: water’s temperature barely changes due to expansion, so a rising blob would stay warm (assuming no mixing). But in reality, lakes are heated from above, so the warm water is already at the top and stays there. ### Contrast with the atmosphere - **Atmosphere:** Heated from the *bottom* by the ground. Warm air is less dense and rises, but as it rises, pressure drops, it expands, and it cools. That’s why air temperature drops with altitude despite warm air rising. The “open system” allows the air to climb kilometres, expand, and cool. - **Lake/Ocean:** Heated from the *top* by the sun. The warm water is already at the surface, floating on colder water. There’s no rising column of warm water that could expand and cool because it’s already where it ends up. The lake’s vertical temperature profile (warm on top, cold below) is perfectly stable. If you were to somehow heat the lake from below, you would see warm water rise in plumes, but those plumes would still be warmer than their surroundings until they mixed. So the lake doesn’t show that “heat rises” on its own; it shows that **less dense fluid floats on more dense fluid**. That’s the same physics as air, but the result is a warm top because the heating is at the top, and water doesn’t cool by expansion. ### A thought experiment to tie it together Imagine a giant, deep atmosphere of water (like an ocean of air) heated from below. That’s exactly our atmosphere with air. Warm air rises, expands, and cools. In a water atmosphere, if it were compressible and heated from below, the temperature would also drop with height due to expansion cooling. But Earth’s water ocean is incompressible and heated from above, so the warm layer floats on top. What you’ve spotted is actually a beautiful confirmation: the principle (buoyancy of warm fluid) is always the same, but the temperature profile depends on *where the heat is added* and whether the fluid cools as it expands. In your house (heated from below, trapped at ceiling) → hot upstairs. In the sky (heated from below, no ceiling, gas expands and cools) → cold upstairs. In a lake (heated from above, incompressible fluid) → warm on top. So again, it’s always the fluid doing the moving—heat just rides along. And that’s why you can have a warm lake surface, a stifling loft, and a freezing‑cold sky all from the same simple law of buoyancy.
