01:13:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’ve hit on a wonderfully visual example, and you’re absolutely right: smoke from a candle or cigarette does drift straight up, even outdoors with no ceiling. That looks like pure “heat rising” happening in the open air. But watching that smoke closely actually reveals the **entire story** — why the plume goes up at first, but also why it doesn’t keep going up forever and why the sky isn’t hot. Let’s break down what that smoke is showing you. ### The smoke is a tracer of hot air, not of heat itself A candle flame heats the air around it. That air expands, becomes less dense, and buoyantly rises — exactly as we’ve described. The smoke particles are tiny, light, and dragged along by that moving air. So what you’re seeing is a **plume of hot air** made visible. It rises quickly for the first few inches because the air inside it is much warmer and lighter than the surrounding room or outdoor air. ### But watch what happens after those first few inches Look closely at a candle or cigarette plume in still air. After rising a short distance, the smoke stops being a thin, fast column. It widens, slows down, wavers, and then often drifts sideways or just dissipates. It does **not** continue in a straight vertical line up to the clouds. Why? Three key processes are at work: 1. **Adiabatic cooling (again).** Even over a few tens of centimetres, the rising plume expands slightly as it goes up. That expansion lowers its temperature. The warmer it was relative to the surrounding air, the more buoyant it was — but as it cools, the buoyancy weakens. 2. **Mixing with cooler ambient air.** The rising plume doesn’t stay isolated. It entrains — pulls in — the surrounding cooler air. This dilutes the heat, further lowering the plume’s temperature. Once the plume’s temperature drops enough, it’s no longer lighter than the air around it, so its upward motion stops. It becomes neutrally buoyant and then simply drifts with any breeze or settles. 3. **Loss of momentum.** The initial upward push is just from buoyancy. As that buoyancy fades, drag from the surrounding air slows the plume down. Without a continuous supply of new hot air pushing from below (the flame), the plume runs out of steam. ### So the smoke plume doesn’t keep rising indefinitely — just like the atmosphere If “heat rises” meant heat always wants to go up regardless of the air, that smoke plume would never stop. It would rise and rise, getting warmer as it climbed, and the sky would be boiling hot. Instead, the plume rises while it’s hot, cools as it rises, and eventually stops. That’s a miniature version of what happens in the atmosphere: warm air near the ground rises (forming thermals), expands, cools, and eventually spreads out at some altitude where its temperature matches the surrounding air. That equilibrium level is where the convective mixing stops, and it’s why the average temperature drops with height — the rising air has cooled, and the air it reaches is even colder. ### Your house gives smoke a ceiling; outdoors gives it room to cool - In a **room with a ceiling**, the warm smoky air from a candle hits a barrier before it has time to cool and mix enough to stop rising. It piles up against the ceiling, making the upper layer warm and smoky. That’s your hot loft, just with visible soot. - **Outdoors**, or in a very tall space like a cathedral, the same plume has room to rise far enough that cooling and mixing take over, and it disperses without forming a hot layer at the top. This is exactly the same contrast between your house and the sky we’ve been talking about, made visible by smoke. ### What about a campfire’s towering column of smoke that goes hundreds of feet up? A large fire produces a much bigger volume of hot air, with a stronger initial buoyancy, so it can rise far higher before cooling and mixing stop it. Even then, it doesn’t go to the stratosphere. It forms a tall plume that eventually bends over with the wind and cools, and the smoke particles disperse. The fact that it goes higher doesn’t change the principle — it’s still hot air rising, expanding, cooling, and mixing, and it eventually stops. ### So yes, smoke shows you hot air rising. But it also shows you why the sky isn’t hot. That little plume is a live demonstration that hot air rises, cools as it goes, and loses its buoyancy. The same process on a planetary scale gives us a warm ground and a cold sky. The smoke makes the invisible air movement visible — but it doesn’t show heat defying gravity. It shows buoyant hot air doing what it does, and then stopping when it’s no longer hot enough to float. And you’re right: if you only looked at the first few inches of the plume, you’d swear “heat rises” was the whole story. It’s only when you follow the plume to its end that you see the full picture — which, of course, is exactly what you’re doing by asking these questions.
