ng about radiators or campfires. Physicists themselves say “hot air rises” all the time. What we do is remember that this is shorthand for “hot air is buoyant and will rise until it either hits a barrier or cools enough by expansion and mixing to stop rising.” The ceiling in your house provides the barrier. In the open sky, the expansion and cooling dominates. So you get two different outcomes from the same starting cause: - In a **trapped space** (your house), the rising air **warms the top**. - In an **unbounded space** (the sky), the rising air **cools as it climbs**, making the upper layers **colder**. Both are true, and both are driven by the same density mechanism. You don't have to discard your experience; you just extend it. ### Why accept the “complicated” version if the simple one works? Because the simple one can’t answer your original question. When you ask “why is the sky cold?”, the answer “because heat rises” makes the exact wrong prediction. To give a satisfying answer, we need the longer story. And once you see how it works, it actually makes the simple rule *more* trustworthy, because you know exactly when and why it applies. You’re absolutely right to hold onto the truth of your warm attic. That’s real. The science doesn’t take it away; it just explains what that hot air would do if you removed the roof—and why the result would be the cold sky that mountain climbers feel. So no, nobody’s asking you to overwrite your direct experience. They’re just providing the missing piece that lets you answer “why is it hot here and cold there?” with a single, consistent idea. That’s not throwing out what your hands feel; it’s making sense of what your hands can’t reach but your mind can now understand.
